A||||2||rodmcneill||Dog bone poly bush. size?||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||12-01-2000||11:43 PM||||Has anyone noticed that when you install poly. bushings in the dogbone that they are actually too wide? With any bushing if its for a front end motor mount or whatever the actual bushing, poly. or rubber should not be clamped itself. This is the reason for the metal tube itself in the middle of the bushing.The bushing should be free to move around,this is the whole reason for bushings. When you clamp down the dogbone bushings you are actually clamping the poly. instead of the center tube like the original. A friend who has access to a lathe said he could cut some of the flange off both poly. bushings. Since it is a split bushing I would probably have to either glue it together in the middle or come up with small metal flanges on both sides of the bushings to keep them together. Any comments?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||12-02-2000||09:14 AM||||Rod, you discovered the dark secret of poly dogbone bushings.  The OEM-style dogbone bushings have a thick tube in the center of the bushing with little teeth that dig-in and grab onto the brackets, which eliminates movement between the tube/bolt and the bracket.  The poly bushings have a thin center tube that is flush with the end of the bushing.  The small amount of clearance between the bolt and the center tube and between the bolt and the bolt holes in the brackets allows motion.  If you don't tighten down hard on the bolt enough to squeeze the bracket against the poly bushing, you will hear clunking when you get on and off the gas. If you do tighten down hard on the bolts, the poly bushings will squeek when you get on and off the gas.  Sure, poly bushings won't wear out like rubber, but they really are not quiet like the OEM stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-02-2000||01:07 PM||||The center sleeve is the same width as the polyurethane on all the bushings I've seen. You do have a center sleeve.... One thing to check with the aftermarket struts is that the poly is meeting in the middle. If it's not then the strut was made wrong or the poly is wrong. pull the sleeve and make sure the poly is touching. A little compression ok, but allot of compression is bad. More than 1/16th I'd look into why.<P>Because the sleeve is the same width as the poly it is not clamped per se. It's that size because if it was smaller it could back out of the strut. This bushing doesn't rotate nor does it need to move latterally. A small amount of side clamping won't hurt at anyrate. There's still enough flex to alow the ends to move in all the ways they need to.<P>Squeak? In the strut? Not in mine, and I've got the 87 4 cyl automatic which shakes the hell out of them. I'd look elsewhere for any squeaks, like loose belts or the begining of a failing alternator waterpump or AC clutch. If you do confirm the poly squeaks in the strut then clean the brackets and give the sides facing the polly a light film of any heavy grease. I'd be suprise if a sqeak was the strut. there's just not the kind of movement there that is known to make polly squeak. Squeaking poly is almost always a control arm or sway bar support bushing that has allot of rotation.<P>cutting down the bushing is of little benifit. As you guessed you'll still need to do something to keep the bushing in place. putting washers on the sleeve isn't any diferant than letting the sides of the bracket do the same job. I don't think glue will work. Poly and all but a very few adhesives doesn't get along. I can't think of any retail adhesive you could buy that would hold it long.<P>The Prothane and Energy Suspension people know what they are doing. If the bushing is one of theirs leave it alone. If it's the "ultimate dog bone" then I believe they are Prothane inserts. <P><BR>AS for putting polly in the strut at all.... With the exception of a big motor upgrade or the torque strut problem described in my cave article I wouldn't use it. As a few other poeple have noted if it's not put in right it can cause damage to the brackets. (Search for recent threads on poly.)||||reg||1
